  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nThis afternoon...Skies are clear except over the mountains where\ncumulus clouds have developed. There is a chance that some showers\nor tstms could develop over the mtns later this afternoon and may\ndrift into the deserts. Temperatures are generally higher than at\nthis time yesterday...especially in the lower deserts where\ntemperatures are as much as 12 degrees higher.\n\nExtensive ridge of high pressure will continue the warming trend through\nThursday, with warmer days and warmer nights. High temperatures \non Thursday will range from the 80s near the coast to 106 to 112 \nfor the Inland Empire with 110 to 116 for the lower deserts. Low \ntemperatures on Thursday will be mostly in the 70s for the coast \nand valleys and in the mid 80s to mid 90s for the lower deserts.\n\nThe highest HeatRisk for the coast and valleys is expected on\nThursday and Friday with widespread Extreme HeatRisk for the \ninland valleys and inland Orange County with Major HeatRisk for \nmost other areas with Moderate HeatRisk limited to the beaches.\n\nMid level monsoonal moisture will remain in place today with a\nslight decrease on Wednesday and a greater decrease on Thursday. A\nslight chance of afternoon showers and thunderstorms, mostly for\nthe mountains, will continue through Wednesday.\n\nFor Saturday through Tuesday, high pressure will begin to weaken \nacross the area as a low pressure system develops near the \nCalifornia coast, strengthening onshore flow at the surface. High\ntemperatures will fall to around 5 degrees above average on \nSunday and a few degrees below average for the mountains and \ninland valleys on Monday. Monday high temperatures will range from\nthe mid 70s to lower 80s near the coast to the 90s for the Inland\nEmpire with 104 to 108 for the lower deserts.",
